+       to lo, 450359962737050 to hi, 8106479329266892 to general. (Neither
+       is exactly 5/5/90 -- 0.05 as a binary64 is a shade over -- but both
+       are wrong by the same amount.)
+
+       What DOES change is which selector reaches which branch, and on
+       whole intervals rather than at the edges: stock's cumulative
+       weights are [0.05; 0.10; 1.0], so hi occupies (0.05, 0.10], while
+       here it occupies [0.95, 1). That remapping is the entire point --
+       it is what makes position 0 order lo < general < hi -- so it is a
+       feature, not a caveat. It is worth stating only because it means
+       a harness that FORCES a selector value can see a different branch:
+       a forced 1.0 takes general under stock and hi here. The ordinary
+       sampler never returns 1.0, so this is unreachable without one.
